  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "What the principal parts are",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "The GCSE word list gives each verb in several forms at once. These are its <b>principal parts</b>. Together they tell you which conjugation the verb belongs to, and later in the course they give you its other tenses."
    },
    {
     "table": {
      "head": [
       "Part",
       "Example",
       "What it is"
      ],
      "rows": [
       [
        "1st",
        "<em>moneo</em>",
        "\"I warn\": the present tense"
       ],
       [
        "2nd",
        "<em>monere</em>",
        "\"to warn\": the infinitive"
       ],
       [
        "3rd",
        "<em>monui</em>",
        "\"I warned\": the perfect tense, in Chapter 4"
       ],
       [
        "4th",
        "<em>monitus</em>",
        "\"warned\": a participle, in Book 2"
       ]
      ]
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "Some verbs have only three parts: <em>venio, venire, veni</em>.",
      "For now you need the first two. They tell you the conjugation.",
      "Nouns work in the same way: the word list gives the genitive, <em>servus, servi</em>, and that shows the declension."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
